1.3.1 Split the plugin into an entry point and a pass
==================================================

Now the toolchain is in place; this part makes LLVM actually run your code. You split the plugin into an entry point, a pass declaration, and a pass implementation, then prove the result loads by having opt find your pass. Every later pass — stats, analysis, and flattening — runs only if this chain works, so each link is built and checked on its own.

### Recall: what a pass is, and how a plugin supplies one

A pass is one program-level inspection or transformation that LLVM runs
over the IR: it receives an object (here, a `Function`), inspects or
changes it, and returns a result describing what it did (Background).
The plugin's job is to make `opt` call it. With
`-load-pass-plugin=...`, `opt` loads the shared library through
`dlopen()` and calls its exported `extern "C"` entry point
`llvmGetPassPluginInfo()`, which returns a `PassPluginLibraryInfo`.
One of the callbacks that structure registers with LLVM's `PassBuilder`
— `registerPipelineParsingCallback` — is where the pipeline name
`minishield-flatten` becomes `FPM.addPass(FlatteningPass())`; lesson 5
traces that registration.

A pass's `run()` receives `llvm::Function &F` and
`llvm::FunctionAnalysisManager &AM`, and returns
`llvm::PreservedAnalyses`: the set of analyses still valid after the pass
ran. `PreservedAnalyses::all()` says nothing was invalidated — correct
for a read-only or stub pass like this one — while
`PreservedAnalyses::none()` says recompute everything.

An analysis is a registered class whose `run()` computes and returns
data instead of modifying the IR. A pass asks for that data with
`AM.getResult<CFGAnalyzer>(F)`; the manager computes it once and caches
it, and the same `PreservedAnalyses` bookkeeping keeps the cache honest.
The `CFGAnalyzer` declaration comes in lesson 15.

