A loop can have several back edges. To count natural loops, include llvm/Analysis/LoopInfo.h and request LoopAnalysis:

```cpp
const auto &LI = AM.getResult<llvm::LoopAnalysis>(F);
result.loopCount = LI.getLoopsInPreorder().size();
```

Initialize loopCount to zero in Result. getLoopsInPreorder() includes nested loops; iterating LI directly visits only top-level loops. LLVM's standard analyses are registered by opt. This count is natural loops, not all possible cycles in a graph with multiple entries.

| Fixture | Natural loops | Back edges |
|---|---|---|
| loop.ll | 1 | 1 |
| phi.ll | 0 | 0 |
| recursion.ll | 0 | 0 |

Recursion cycles between function calls, not blocks within a function. Print loops and back edges inside the one-line statistics summary if the table formatter should include them. Extra CFG diagnostic lines are filtered out by the table runner.

Checkpoint: explain why an analysis result is requested by reference, why dominance argument order matters, and why back-edge count is not automatically a loop count.

### Model answer

1. The manager computes the analysis and owns its result, so `getResult<llvm::LoopAnalysis>(F)` hands back a reference to a cached object: binding `const auto &` lets the pass read `loopCount` without copying the loop structure into its own frame, and the result stays alive until the manager invalidates it — which the pass’s `PreservedAnalyses` return governs.
2. `dominates(D, B)` asks whether D dominates B, so the dominator comes first. The back-edge test needs “does the destination dominate the source”, which is why the loop writes `DT.dominates(successor, &BB)` — destination first, as the deep dive states. Swapped, the call would ask whether the source dominates the destination: the forward-edge relation, false on the loop’s own back edge (in `loop.ll`, `header` dominates `body`, not the other way round), so the test would find no back edges at all.
3. A back edge is an edge into a header; a natural loop is the set of blocks flowing around it. One loop can be entered by several back edges — any structure with two paths that both return to the header contributes two back edges to a single loop — so the back-edge count counts edges while `LI.getLoopsInPreorder()` counts loops (nested loops included; iterating `LI` directly visits only the top level). The two agree only when every loop has exactly one back edge, as in `loop.ll` (1 and 1); in a graph with multiple entries, cycles that are not natural loops exist in neither count.

### Deep dive: why `info.block = &BB` but `push_back(successor)` needs no `&`

In the basic analysis you do two different things with blocks: you **walk**
them and you **store** their addresses. The walk uses a reference
(`const llvm::BasicBlock &BB`), while the stored member `info.block` is a
pointer (`const llvm::BasicBlock *`). Assigning the block to a pointer is a
type mismatch, so store the block's address: `info.block = &BB;` — here `&`
is the address-of operator, not a reference declaration. The successor loop is
the mirror image: `llvm::successors(&BB)` already yields block pointers, so
`successor` is a pointer and you store it as-is with `push_back(successor)`;
writing `push_back(&successor)` would store a `BasicBlock **`, one level too
deep. The full reference-versus-pointer treatment is in the C++ concepts
reference (section 1.7.1).

