1.6.1 LLVM’s in-memory object model
===================================

A pass does not edit a printed `.ll` file as text. It walks LLVM's in-memory
C++ objects. Knowing which object contains which other object tells you where
to start, what a loop gives you, and which parent owns an instruction you may
erase.

| LLVM object | Contains or represents | How MiniShield uses it |
| --- | --- | --- |
| `LLVMContext` | Shared, uniqued types and constants plus IR-wide support data | Get it from `F.getContext()` when creating types, constants, or blocks. |
| `Module` | A whole IR unit: functions, globals, declarations, and module metadata | `opt` loads a `.ll` file into a Module before the pass pipeline runs. |
| `Function` | A function definition or declaration, with its arguments and basic blocks; it is also a module-level `GlobalValue` and a `Value` | Function passes receive a Function; declarations have no body to flatten. |
| `BasicBlock` | An ordered list of instructions with one entry and a final terminator | The flattener assigns state IDs to blocks and rewrites their terminators. |
| `Instruction` | One operation in a basic block; result-producing instructions are also Values | The pass scans instructions for PHIs, calls, allocations, and values crossing block boundaries. |
| `Value` | The common interface for typed operands such as arguments, constants, globals, and instruction results | IRBuilder returns Values that later instructions consume; Values also have uses. |
| `Type` | The description of a value's shape, such as `i32`, `ptr`, or `void` | The eligibility guard checks result types; the state slot is i32 storage. |

The containment chain is `Module → Function → BasicBlock → Instruction`. It is
a useful starting point, but not every object is stored in that chain:
arguments belong to a Function, constants are shared/uniqued through an
`LLVMContext`, and types describe values rather than being executable objects.

### Walk the objects in the same order

```cpp
#include "llvm/IR/BasicBlock.h"
#include "llvm/IR/Function.h"
#include "llvm/IR/Instruction.h"
#include "llvm/IR/Module.h"
#include "llvm/IR/Type.h"

void inspectModule(llvm::Module &M) {
    for (llvm::Function &F : M) {
        if (F.isDeclaration())
            continue;

        for (llvm::BasicBlock &BB : F) {
            for (llvm::Instruction &I : BB) {
                llvm::Type *resultType = I.getType();
                (void)resultType; // Ask what shape this instruction returns.
            }
        }
    }
}
```

Each range supplies a reference to an existing object: the Module yields
Functions, a Function yields BasicBlocks, and a BasicBlock yields Instructions.
The loops do not copy the IR objects. The nested traversal mirrors the parent
structure, which is why a Function pass naturally starts at `F` and visits its
blocks and instructions.

### Read one LLVM function as C++ objects

```llvm
define i32 @add_one(i32 %x) {
entry:
  %sum = add i32 %x, 1
  ret i32 %sum
}
```

`@add_one` is a Function object and module-level symbol. `%x` is an Argument;
arguments are Values. `entry:` names a BasicBlock. In C++, `BasicBlock`
derives from `Value`, but the printed label is a control-flow destination, not
an ordinary integer or pointer datum. `%sum = add ...` is an Instruction;
because `add` produces an i32 result, it is also a Value used by `ret`. The
literal `1` is a typed constant Value, not an Instruction. `i32` is a Type
describing the shape of values; it is not a value itself.

LLVM printer punctuation does not map one-to-one to C++ classes. `@` names a
module-level symbol; `%` names local values and block labels, disambiguated by
syntax and position. A Value's type describes its operand or result shape,
and its use-list records where it is consumed. Parent containers own their
child IR nodes, so erasing an Instruction invalidates that instruction
pointer. A pass must not reuse a collected pointer after erasure.

A `BasicBlock *` is an address of the block object owned by its Function; it
is not a copy of the block and not its printed name. The string `entry` is a
label for humans and IR references, while pointer identity is what a
`DenseMap` uses to key state IDs. A C++ reference such as `BasicBlock &BB` is
an alias for an existing object; a pointer-taking API needs `&BB` to receive
that object's address.

